@PACK Gold & Rocks
@ENTRY panning|How to Pan for Gold
Gold is nineteen times heavier than water and about seven times heavier than the sand it hides in. Every trick in panning comes from that one fact: shake the pan so everything is moving, and the gold sinks while the light stuff rides up where you can wash it off.

Fill the pan about two-thirds with gravel, hold it underwater, and break up every clod with your fingers. Pull out the big rocks after rinsing them over the pan. Then shake the pan side to side, hard, ten or fifteen seconds. That's what settles the gold. Tilt the pan away from you and sweep the top layer of light sand out with a gentle circular wash. Level up, shake again, wash again. Repeat until only a spoonful of dark sand is left.

The finish is the fun part: an inch of water in the pan, swirl it slowly, and let the water walk across the black sand like a broom. Gold stays put as a bright tail behind the sweep. Pick out flakes with a wet fingertip or a snuffer bottle. If you lose color, stop washing and shake more. Nobody shakes too much; everybody washes too fast.
@END
@ENTRY reading|Reading a River
Gold moves during floods and only during floods, and it drops out of the current the instant the water slows. So you are not looking for gold; you are looking for slow water in a flood. Inside bends, behind boulders, below rapids where the river widens, the downstream side of gravel bars: those are pay streaks.

Bedrock is the bottom of the river's pocket. Gold works its way down through gravel for years until something solid stops it, so cracks, crevices, and clay layers on bedrock hold the oldest and best gold. A crevice the width of your finger can hold more color than a yard of loose gravel above it.

Look up, too. Rivers used to run higher on the valley walls. Old gravel benches thirty feet above today's water were the riverbed once, and the old-timers called them bench placers for good reason.
@END
@ENTRY blacksand|Black Sand & Fool's Gold
That heavy black sand in the bottom of every pan is mostly magnetite, an iron mineral, and it is your friend: gold and black sand settle together, so where one collects, the other does. No black sand after good shaking usually means the spot doesn't concentrate heavies at all.

Fool's gold is pyrite or mica, and the river sorts the confusion out for you if you watch closely. Pyrite is brassy, has square crystal faces, and shatters when pressed with a knife tip. Mica flashes bright then vanishes as it turns, because it's a flat flake reflecting the sun. Gold is gold-colored from every angle, dents instead of breaking, and stays put in the pan when everything else dances.

A cheap magnet in a plastic bag makes black sand cleanup easy: touch, lift, shake the magnet clean over a bucket, repeat. What's left is the good stuff.
@END
@ENTRY rockhounding|Rockhounding Basics
Rockhounding is just hiking with a purpose and a bucket. Road cuts, dry washes, mine dumps, and eroding hillsides expose fresh rock that weather hasn't rotted yet. After a hard rain is prime time; the desert rearranges its display case every storm.

The kit is short: a crack hammer or rock pick, safety glasses (rock chips fly, always), gloves, newspaper for wrapping finds, and water to wet a surface, because a wet rock shows its true color and pattern like a polished one.

Know the rules before you dig. Collecting is generally allowed on most public land for personal use in small amounts, but national parks are strictly look-don't-take, and claims belong to the claim holder. A quick check saves an awkward conversation.
@END
@ENTRY identify|Knowing What You Found
Three quick tests identify most rocks. Hardness: your fingernail is about 2.5, a copper coin about 3, a knife blade about 5.5, quartz 7. Whatever scratches what tells you where the mystery rock sits. Streak: rub the rock on the unglazed back of a ceramic tile; the powder color is more honest than the rock's surface. Weight: heft it. Ore minerals feel heavier than they look.

Quartz is the great impostor and the great signpost. Milky white veins of it carried the hot fluids that deposited gold, so gold country is quartz country. Agate and jasper are quartz too, just colored and banded; they take a beautiful polish.

Geodes look like dull gray or brown cannonballs, lighter than they should be, often with a bumpy cauliflower surface. The only way to know is to crack one, and the only rule is: tap gently around the equator, don't smash. Half the fun is that nobody on Earth has seen the inside before you.
@END
@ENTRY crystals|Crystals & Gems to Hunt
Every crystal is a shape a mineral takes when it has room to grow, and each mineral only knows one shape. Quartz grows six-sided points. Garnets grow twelve-sided balls, like tiny soccer balls, and weather whole out of mica schist so you can pick them from the gravel of a creek. Topaz forms stubby prisms in cavities of rhyolite and can be found by simply walking washes after rain and watching for glint.

Obsidian is volcanic glass, jet black, sometimes with a golden or rainbow sheen. It fractures in smooth curved shells, which is why every edge is razor sharp; handle it like broken glass, because that's exactly what it is.

Fire agate, sunstones, opal, and petrified wood each have famous localities in the West, many with public fee-dig sites where a few dollars buys a day of digging and you keep everything you find. Fee digs are the best training ground there is: you learn what fresh, unweathered material looks like straight from the ground.
@END
@ENTRY history|The Gold Rush Story
When gold turned up at Sutter's Mill in 1848, the news emptied towns on every coast and put three hundred thousand people on the trail west. Most never struck it rich. The reliable fortunes went to the people selling shovels, eggs, and blue jeans; a miner might pay a day's findings for a loaf of bread in a boom camp.

The easy gold, the nuggets lying in streambeds, was gone within a few years. Everything after that was work: diverting whole rivers, tunneling into mountains, washing hillsides away with water cannons. Ghost towns mark the spots where the gold ran out faster than the optimism.

Here's the encouraging part for you: the old-timers only got the gold that had eroded out by their day. Mountains keep eroding. Every flood pulls a little more color down into the gravels, which is why a patient kid with a pan can still find real gold in the same rivers, a century and a half later.
@END
